    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d3">

                <pb facs="0105" n="3" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg3"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4 General Committee (Arms)/81:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the American Adviser</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Mayer</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-01-15T20:00:00-05:00">January 15, 1935—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>466. Your 962, January 15, 1 p.m. We approve, in general, the views and the
                    procedure outlined in the penultimate paragraph, but do not want to assume the
                    position of forcing the hands of Great Britain and France. Your best approach
                    would seem to be to state that while we wish in no manner to interfere with or
                    hamper any major political negotiations, we do not quite understand why
                    simultaneous work of a purely technical nature at Geneva should have this
                    effect, and that we would view with great regret any undue delay in the
                    resumption of the technical work of the Committees.</p>
                <p>Your 963, January 15, 4 p.m.<anchor n="12" xml:id="d3-f1"/> Approved.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
